[
  {
    "id": "heldout-1",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "h1-a",
        "prompt": "A community garden shares one water tank among six plots. Volunteers record the tank level each morning, but rainfall and watering times vary. Design a simple weekly watering plan that avoids emptying the tank while keeping seedlings alive. Explain which observations should change the plan and how to handle a missing measurement. Distinguish a useful estimate from a guaranteed supply. Give a small example involving a dry week, and explain why simply dividing the tank equally every day might waste water or neglect a plot with newly planted seeds."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-b",
        "prompt": "A museum is moving fragile objects from storage into an exhibition. Each object must be photographed, inspected, packed, transported, and checked on arrival. Only one photographer and two trained handlers are available, and some boxes require both handlers. Describe a practical workflow that avoids lost records and unnecessary waiting. Explain how to handle an object whose inspection finds damage, and how the team should recover if a transport trip is cancelled. Identify which steps can overlap and which must remain in order. Do not assume that every object takes the same time."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-c",
        "prompt": "A program writes a configuration file that another process reads periodically. Sometimes the reader sees half of a newly written file and fails. Propose a robust update procedure using a temporary file and an atomic replacement. Explain what should happen when writing fails, when the application crashes before replacement, and when two writers attempt an update at once. Separate protection against partial files from protection against lost updates. Include a brief example of the sequence of operations and discuss what additional care is needed if durability after a power failure is required."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-d",
        "prompt": "A school wants to compare two ways of teaching vocabulary. One class uses flash cards in the morning and another class plays a word game after lunch. Their teachers, ages, and attendance differ. Explain why the final quiz scores alone cannot establish which method works better. Propose a modest study that would make the comparison more useful without excluding pupils who need extra support. Discuss practice effects, missing quizzes, and the difference between remembering words tomorrow and remembering them next month. Give an example of a conclusion the study could support and one it could not."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-e",
        "prompt": "A mobile application downloads a large map over an unreliable connection. Users can pause the download, restart the application, and switch between networks. Describe a design that resumes safely without treating a damaged partial file as a complete map. Explain how to identify which version is being downloaded, verify downloaded pieces, and publish the completed file. Discuss what to do when the remote map changes during the download. Compare the role of a checksum with the role of a version identifier, and explain why storing only the number of bytes already received is insufficient."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-f",
        "prompt": "A small bakery prepares bread and pastries using one oven with two shelves. Some recipes require different temperatures, and opening the door delays baking. Customer orders arrive throughout the morning, with a few promised pickup times. Outline a scheduling approach that balances oven utilization with reliable pickup times. Explain why grouping every recipe of the same temperature together may be unfair to later orders. Describe how the baker should revise the plan after an urgent order or a failed batch. Use a short example and identify assumptions that would need real timing measurements."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-g",
        "prompt": "An online form lets people reserve seats for a workshop. Two people can submit a request for the last seat at nearly the same moment. Explain how the server can prevent overbooking while still handling retries after a network timeout. Distinguish checking whether a seat is available from actually reserving it. Describe the role of a transaction and an idempotency identifier. Discuss what response a retry should receive when the original request succeeded but the reply was lost, and how expired temporary reservations should be released without cancelling a completed reservation."
      },
      {
        "id": "h1-h",
        "prompt": "A neighborhood group measures noise near a construction site using inexpensive sensors. Readings differ between devices and sometimes jump when a truck passes directly beside one sensor. Explain how to summarize the measurements without hiding brief loud events or treating every spike as a permanent change. Discuss calibration, sensor placement, missing data, and time of day. Suggest a transparent way to compare a week before and a week after a change in working hours. Describe which limitations should accompany the results and why a single average cannot answer every concern residents may have."
      }
    ],
