  {
    "id": "heldout-2",
    "items": [
      {
        "id": "h2-a",
        "prompt": "A volunteer kitchen receives donated vegetables with uncertain quantities and freshness. It must plan meals for forty people using a limited refrigerator and two cooking stations. Describe a practical planning process that reduces waste while keeping meals predictable. Explain which ingredients should be used first, how to handle a late donation, and what information volunteers should record at the end of the day. Discuss the tradeoff between preparing everything early and waiting for a more accurate attendance count. Give an example of a fallback meal and identify assumptions that should be checked rather than guessed."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-b",
        "prompt": "A service processes payment notifications delivered by an external provider. Notifications may arrive twice, out of order, or after a long delay. Explain how to maintain a reliable local record without charging a customer twice or moving an order backward to an older state. Describe what identifiers should be stored and when processing should be acknowledged. Discuss how to recover if the service crashes after changing the database but before acknowledging a notification. Separate duplicate detection from ordering rules, and give a small example with three notifications arriving in an unexpected order."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-c",
        "prompt": "A town tests a new bus timetable on one route during a rainy month. Passenger counts increase, but a nearby train service also experiences frequent cancellations. Explain what evidence is needed before attributing the increase to the timetable. Propose a comparison that uses existing observations while acknowledging their limits. Discuss differences between peak and off-peak travel, school holidays, and passengers transferring from trains. Describe how the town could report uncertainty honestly, and give an example of a useful operational decision that does not require claiming the timetable caused every additional journey."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-d",
        "prompt": "A desktop editor periodically saves recovery copies while the user continues typing. Saving a large document can take several seconds, and the user may close the document or rename it during that time. Design a recovery system that keeps a consistent snapshot without blocking every keystroke. Explain how to associate a saved copy with the correct document version, how to avoid replacing a newer recovery copy with an older one, and how to clean up abandoned temporary files. Discuss what should happen after a crash when several incomplete or complete copies are present."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-e",
        "prompt": "A science club is comparing two materials used to insulate a cup of warm water. Cups have slightly different shapes, room temperature changes during the afternoon, and the thermometer takes time to settle. Explain how to design a fair small experiment using equipment the club already has. Discuss starting temperatures, measurement order, repeated trials, and how to record unexpected events. Describe a simple graph that would help interpret cooling over time. Explain why one final temperature reading is weaker evidence than a consistent pattern across comparable trials, without claiming that more decimal places guarantee accuracy."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-f",
        "prompt": "A warehouse robot receives instructions to collect items from several shelves before returning to a packing station. Some aisles are narrow, and another robot may temporarily block a route. Describe a planning strategy that avoids repeatedly abandoning useful progress whenever a delay occurs. Explain which information should trigger replanning and which small delays can be tolerated. Discuss how to prevent two robots from waiting forever for each other. Give an example involving a blocked aisle and a priority order, and distinguish a shortest route from a plan that reliably finishes on time."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-g",
        "prompt": "A web dashboard combines information from three services that refresh at different rates. Users sometimes see a total that does not match the displayed component values because updates arrive separately. Explain several ways to present the data consistently. Compare taking a shared snapshot, displaying timestamps, and delaying an update until related values are ready. Discuss what happens when one service becomes unavailable or returns old information. Describe a sensible interface behavior that helps users understand freshness without suggesting that data from different moments describes one exact instant."
      },
      {
        "id": "h2-h",
        "prompt": "A local repair cooperative wants to keep a useful stock of spare parts but has little storage space. A few parts are used every week, while others are rarely needed but take a long time to obtain. Describe an initial stocking policy and how it should change as records accumulate. Explain why average monthly usage is not enough to decide every quantity. Discuss delivery delays, substitutes, shared parts, and the cost of an unfinished repair. Give a small example of two parts that deserve different policies even if their annual usage is similar."
      }
    ],
    "concurrency": 8,
